        -:    1:// Concurrency-model properties (spec §7 and §10.2).
        -:    2://
        -:    3:// The conformance vectors run one operation at a time against the service, so
        -:    4:// they exercise the routing but say nothing about the queue and completion
        -:    5:// machinery underneath, which is exactly the part §7 specifies and the part the
        -:    6:// thesis compares between the two languages. These tests drive the service from
        -:    7:// several threads at once and assert the guarantees the spec makes about it:
        -:    8:// blocking backpressure loses nothing (§7.4-7.5), a shard executes its queue in
        -:    9:// FIFO order (§10.2 O1), and each request's completion goes to its own caller
        -:   10:// exactly once (§10.2 O3).
        -:   11://
        -:   12:// Build this binary under ThreadSanitizer to also check data-race freedom; the
        -:   13:// Rust counterpart (rust/tests/concurrency.rs) needs no sanitizer for that.
